<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edwin Lutyens</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Edwin Lutyens was an English architect known for country houses, war memorials and the planning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-baker">Herbert Baker</a> he designed the new imperial city, and his Viceroy's House, now Rashtrapati Bhavan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Gate combine classical forms with Indian elements. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he designed the Cenotaph in Whitehall, the national memorial to the dead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edwin-lutyens.jpg" alt="Edwin Lutyens" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Weaver, Lawrence, 1876-1930 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edwin_Lutyens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edwin Lutyens was born in Kensington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the tenth of thirteen children of Mary Theresa Gallwey and Captain Charles Augustus Henry Lutyens, an English soldier and painter. He grew up in Thursley, Surrey. Rheumatic fever in childhood kept him from formal schooling. He studied architecture at the South Kensington School of Art from 1885 to 1887, then joined the practice of Ernest George and Harold Peto, where he first met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-baker">Herbert Baker</a>. In 1888 he set up his own practice; his first commission was a private house at Crooksbury, Farnham.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Crooksbury he met the garden designer Gertrude Jekyll, for whom he began the house at Munstead Wood in 1896. Their partnership shaped many of his country houses, which set hardy planting within terraces, stairs and balustrades. Edward Hudson's magazine Country Life made his houses widely known, and Hudson commissioned him for Lindisfarne Castle. Around 1900 his style turned to a more conventional classicism, seen in Julius Drewe's Castle Drogo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a> was chosen in 1911 to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> as the seat of the British Indian government, and Lutyens worked on it from 1912 to 1930. He devised his own classical order, the Delhi Order, and based his layout on Mughal water gardens. The city was built of local red sandstone. He quarrelled repeatedly with the Viceroy, Viceroy Hardinge, who wanted Indian elements where Lutyens wanted classical architecture. He was knighted in 1918 and made a Knight Commander of the Order of the Indian Empire in 1930.</p><p>He was also one of three principal architects of the Imperial War Graves Commission and designed the Stone of Remembrance used in larger cemeteries and the Memorial to the Missing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somme-river">Somme</a> at Thiepval. The Cenotaph began in 1919 as a temporary structure for the Allied Victory Parade; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">Lloyd George</a> proposed a low catafalque, but Lutyens suggested a taller monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lutyens was diagnosed with cancer in the early 1940s and died on 1 January 1944. His ashes lie in the crypt of St Paul's Cathedral. He received the Royal Gold Medal of the Royal Institute of British Architects in 1921 and the Gold Medal of the American Institute of Architects in 1925. His design for a vast Roman Catholic cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, begun in 1933, was halted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and only its crypt was completed. In 2015 all 44 of his surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> memorials in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> were listed for legal protection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edwin Lutyens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished among the new middle classes: Rabindranath Tagore won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1913, and the architecture of New Delhi, designed by <b>Edwin Lutyens</b> and Herbert Baker and inaugurated in 1931, still houses the government of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1917" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edwin Lutyens"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
